          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>T.S. Eliot's Early Life</strong><br>\nThomas Stearns Eliot, known as T.S. Eliot, was born on September 26, 1888, in St. Louis, Missouri, USA. He was the youngest of seven children in a distinguished family. Eliot was educated at Smith Academy in St. Louis and later attended Harvard University. During his early years, he was influenced by writers like Dante and Shakespeare, which shaped his literary development.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Move to England</strong><br>\nIn 1914, Eliot moved to England and studied at Oxford University. The outbreak of World War I made it difficult for him to return to the United States, prompting him to settle in England permanently. He worked as a schoolteacher and later in a bank, while he continued to write and publish his poetry.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Poetry and Style</strong><br>\nEliot's poetry is known for its difficult and obscure style, which often reflects the complexity of the modern world. His work is characterized by deep cultural references and a range of influences from the metaphysical poets to French symbolists. \"The Love Song of J. Alfred Prufrock\" is one of his first notable works, showcasing his innovative approach to poetry.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
