          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Waste Land</strong><br>\nIn 1922, Eliot published \"The Waste Land,\" which became one of the most famous poems of the 20th century. The poem's fragmentation, rich imagery, and exploration of despair captured the post-war disillusionment of the era. Its publication marked a significant moment in modern literature, establishing Eliot as a leading figure in the literary world.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Literary Criticism</strong><br>\nBesides poetry, Eliot made significant contributions to literary criticism. His essays on poetry, culture, and society are highly regarded. He argued for a new approach to literary evaluation, emphasizing tradition and the impersonality of the poet. Eliot's critical work influenced the way literature was studied and appreciated.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Plays and Dramatic Works</strong><br>\nEliot also wrote plays to bring his complex ideas to a wider audience. His first play, \"Murder in the Cathedral,\" was written for the Canterbury Festival in 1935. He continued to write plays such as \"The Family Reunion\" and \"The Cocktail Party,\" which were both critically acclaimed and popular among audiences.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
