            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Nobel Prize in Literature</strong><br>\nIn 1948, T.S. Eliot was awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature for his outstanding, pioneer contribution to present-day poetry. The Nobel Prize recognized his profound effect on the direction of modern poetry and his role in reshaping contemporary literature.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Life and Legacy</strong><br>\nIn his later years, Eliot continued to work as an editor and publisher, influencing the work of other poets and writers. He died on January 4, 1965, in London, England. His legacy lives on, as his poetry and critical essays continue to be studied and revered for their artistic depth and cultural insight.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Influence on Modern Literature</strong><br>\nEliot's influence on modern literature is immeasurable. His style and use of language pushed the boundaries of poetry and opened new possibilities for expression and meaning. His work not only defines the modernist movement but also continues to challenge and inspire poets and readers today.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
